<title>GERMANY: Lufthansa cancels most French flights.</title>
<headline>Lufthansa cancels most French flights.</headline>
<dateline>FRANKFURT 1996-10-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>Deutsche Lufthansa said it has cancelled all but four of its 50 flights from France on Thursday because of the French air traffic controllers strike.</p>
<p>The German airline is flying only four Paris Charles de Gaulle to Frankfurt services, Lufthansa spokesman Gerd Leidinger told Reuters.</p>
<p>In addition, flights from Nice are being flown out of Genoa with shuttle buses running between the two cities, he added.</p>
<p>Some 6,000 Lufthansa passengers have had their travel plans disrupted but it was too early to say how much revenue Lufthansa had lost through the strike, Leidinger said.</p>
<p>Flights passing through French airspace, mostly to Spain, are being delayed by 20 to 60 minutes, he added.</p>
<p>Lufthansa flies from major German airports to Paris, Lyon, Strasbourg, Toulouse, Marseille, Nice and Bastia under its current summer flight schedule.</p>
